1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
This is a walkthrough of Heritage Short: a pipeline that turns one
JSON storyboard into a finished short film. The film is an Ethiopian
folk tale, narrated in Amharic with English subtitles.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The film is a motion comic. Generated panels are cut and moved like
a comic page: camera pushes, over-the-shoulder cuts, speech
balloons, sound effects, word-by-word captions. There is no video
model and no GPU in this film; the picture is drawn on the CPU in
about four minutes.

3
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The same timeline renders twice. The landscape version is a comic
page: the panel strip stays centred at full height, the spoken
Amharic runs down the left margin, the English down the right. One
film data file, one audio master, two layouts.

4
00:00:45,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Everything starts from storyboard.json: ten beats, each with a
duration, an image prompt, a motion prompt, the narration and its
English line, a reason, and a seed. Beside it, panels.json says how
each beat is cut. Every stage validates this against the schema
before it runs, so a broken contract stops the build before a model
is called.

5
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:31,000
The pipeline is nine stages. Narration records ten lines. Two cast
portraits anchor the characters. Keyframes and sixteen interaction
panels are generated from prompts. The build turns them into a
timeline, the score is synthesised from that timeline, and the
picture is rendered in both ratios and muxed with one audio master.

6
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:51,000
One command runs all of it. On a fresh clone with no API keys, both
films rebuild from the committed cache: zero model calls,
thirty-nine cache hits, about nine minutes including the Rust build.

7
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:17,000
The hardest problem was keeping a character the same person across
twenty-six cuts without wrecking the shot. A reference portrait
holds the face, but it is also a composition prior: attached to a
wide shot it produced a giant head over a tiny scene. So: anchor the
close-ups, and describe the wides in full.

8
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Here is the live part. I change one image prompt — one shot, one
line of the storyboard.

9
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Then I rerun the keyframe stage. Ten jobs are served from the cache.
One image is drawn: seventeen point nine seconds, about a third of a
cent. The log records exactly one call, with its seed.

10
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Every call lands in runs.jsonl with its model, parameters, seed,
latency and cost. The whole project, every iteration included, came
to about sixty-five cents at list prices. The assets in the final
film: eleven cents.

11
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:37,000
What this is not. There is no video model in the film: the clip
route is implemented and untested. There is no music model: the
score is a bed my own code synthesises. And the audience test the
brief asks for was not run — that is written down in DESIGN.md
rather than invented.

12
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:49,000
The repository, both films and this transcript are linked below.
Every number in the documentation can be checked against the run
log.
